module

public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.FlatSquareZeroThickening
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.ImageProductSubmodule
import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-! # The multiplication map of a square-zero ideal and the ideal product

Let `f : O' ⟶ O` be a morphism of sheaves of commutative rings on a site `(C, J)`, let `I` be a
sheaf of `O`-modules included into `O'` by `i`, and let `G` be a sheaf of `O'`-modules. Two
objects are attached to this data:

* the multiplication map `nu : I ⊗_{O'} G ⟶ G` of
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.multiplicationMap`, which Stacks 08M4 is stated with,
  and
* the ideal product `I·G`, the submodule
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.imageProductSubmodule i G` generated by the local products
  `i(z) • m`.

This file proves that a morphism out of `G` kills one exactly when it kills the other, and
deduces that `nu` and the inclusion of `I·G` have the same cokernels.

## Why this is what Stacks 08MQ needs

Stacks 08M4 asks for its reduction `G ⟶ F` as a *cokernel of `nu`*. In the thickening setting
of Stacks 08MQ the reduction arrives instead as a cokernel of the inclusion of an ideal
product — that is the shape of
`Deformation.ToposThickening.idealProductShortComplex_shortExact`, which exhibits
`0 ⟶ I·G ⟶ G ⟶ i_*i^*G ⟶ 0` — so the two presentations have to be identified before 08M4 can
consume the second. `cokernelCofork_multiplicationMap_isColimit` is that identification.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.imageProductSubmodule_ι_comp_eq_zero`: a morphism
  killing `nu` kills `I·G`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero`: a morphism killing
  `I·G` kills `nu`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero_iff`: the two conditions
  are equivalent.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.cokernelCofork_multiplicationMap_isColimit`: a cokernel
  of the inclusion of `I·G` is a cokernel of `nu`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.multiplicationMapIsoIdealProduct`: an **injective** `nu`
  is an isomorphism onto `I·G`, over `G`. This is the conversion clause (2) of Stacks 08MQ
  needs, 08M4 delivering the injectivity of `nu` and 08MQ asking for an isomorphism onto `I·G`.

## Implementation notes

**The two directions are not symmetric, and only one of them is cheap.**

The direction `multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero` — from `I·G` to `nu` — is a computation on pure
tensors: a morphism out of the sheafified tensor product is determined by its values on the
images of pure tensors, through the sheafification adjunction `nu` is itself defined by, and on
`z ⊗ m` the value is `i(z) • m`, which lies in `I·G` by construction. This is the argument of
`multiplicationMap_tmul`'s consumers in
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.FlatSquareZeroThickening`.

The direction `imageProductSubmodule_ι_comp_eq_zero` — from `nu` to `I·G` — cannot be done that
way, because a section of `I·G` is only *locally* a sum of products `i(z) • m` and so is not in
the range of `nu` on the nose. What makes it work is that the kernel of the morphism is itself a
*sheaf* submodule of `G`: it contains every generator `i(z) • m = nu(z ⊗ m)`, so the universal
property of `I·G` as the smallest sheaf submodule containing the generators
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.imageProductSubmodule_le`) puts all of `I·G` inside it. No locality
argument is made by hand; it is discharged by the submodule's `isSheaf` field.

This is why the comparison is stated for a morphism of `O'`-modules and not for one that is
linear only over a smaller ring: over a smaller ring the kernel is not an `O'`-submodule and the
universal property does not apply. The version for a morphism linear over a smaller ring is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.apply_eq_zero_of_mem_imageProductSubmodule`, which has to build the
relevant `O'`-submodule by hand instead.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 08M4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08M4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08MQ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08MQ)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TensorProduct Opposite

universe v u w

namespace Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
  [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{w} RingCat.{w})]
  [J.HasSheafCompose (forget AddCommGrpCat.{w})]
  [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{w}]
  [HasWeakSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{w}]

/-- The sectionwise kernel of a morphism of sheaves of modules, as a submodule of its source.
The family of kernels is stable under restriction by naturality, and its membership condition is
local because the target is a sheaf, hence separated: a section all of whose restrictions along
a covering vanish already vanishes.

This is the same construction as
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.kernelSubmodule`, kept private here because that one
is stated only for a site whose objects, morphisms and sections share a universe, which the
setting of Stacks 08M4 does not. -/
private noncomputable def kernelSub {R : Sheaf J RingCat.{w}} {M N : SheafOfModules.{w} R}
    (g : M ⟶ N) : M.Submodule where
  toSubmodule :=
    { obj U := LinearMap.ker (g.val.app U).hom
      map {U V} p := by
        intro s hs
        change g.val.app V (M.val.map p s) = 0
        rw [PresheafOfModules.naturality_apply, show g.val.app U s = 0 from hs]
        exact map_zero _ }
  isSheaf := by
    intro U s hs
    change g.val.app U s = 0
    have hN : Presieve.IsSheaf J (N.val.presheaf ⋙ CategoryTheory.forget AddCommGrpCat.{w}) :=
      (isSheaf_iff_isSheaf_of_type J _).mp
        (GrothendieckTopology.HasSheafCompose.isSheaf _ N.isSheaf)
    refine (hN _ hs).isSeparatedFor.ext fun V p hp => ?_
    change N.val.map p.op (g.val.app U s) = N.val.map p.op 0
    rw [← PresheafOfModules.naturality_apply]
    change g.val.app (op V) (M.val.map p.op s) = 0 at hp
    rwa [map_zero]

omit [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat RingCat)] [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat]
  [HasWeakSheafify J AddCommGrpCat] in
private lemma mem_kernelSub {R : Sheaf J RingCat.{w}} {M N : SheafOfModules.{w} R} (g : M ⟶ N)
    (U : Cᵒᵖ) (s : M.val.obj U) : s ∈ (kernelSub g).obj U ↔ g.val.app U s = 0 :=
  Iff.rfl

variable {O' O : Sheaf J CommRingCat.{w}} (f : O' ⟶ O)
  (I : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O))
  (G : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O'))
  (i : (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (ringMap f)).obj I ⟶
    SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O'))

/-- **A morphism out of `G` that kills the multiplication map kills the ideal product `I·G`.**

The kernel of such a morphism is a sheaf submodule of `G` containing every generator
`i(z) • m = nu(z ⊗ m)` of `I·G`, so it contains `I·G` by the universal property of the latter as
the smallest such submodule. The locality of the membership condition — the point at which a
section of `I·G` stops being a sum of products — is carried by that universal property and is
not argued here. -/
theorem imageProductSubmodule_ι_comp_eq_zero
    {Z : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O')}
    (g : G ⟶ Z) (hg : multiplicationMap f I G i ≫ g = 0) :
    (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι ≫ g = 0 := by
  have hgen : ∀ (U : Cᵒᵖ) (z : ((SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (ringMap f)).obj I).val.obj U)
      (m : G.val.obj U),
      (show (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O').obj.obj U from
        i.val.app U z) • m ∈ (kernelSub g).obj U := by
    intro U z m
    rw [mem_kernelSub]
    rw [← multiplicationMap_tmul f I G i U (show I.val.obj U from z) m]
    change (multiplicationMap f I G i ≫ g).val.app U _ = 0
    rw [hg]
    rfl
  have hle : imageProductSubmodule i G ≤ kernelSub g := imageProductSubmodule_le i hgen
  exact SheafOfModules.hom_ext (PresheafOfModules.hom_ext fun U ↦
    ModuleCat.hom_ext (LinearMap.ext fun s ↦ hle U s.2))

omit [J.HasSheafCompose (forget AddCommGrpCat)] in
/-- **A morphism out of `G` that kills the ideal product `I·G` kills the multiplication map.**

A morphism out of the sheafified tensor product is determined by its values on the images of
pure tensors, through the sheafification adjunction the multiplication map is itself defined by;
on `z ⊗ m` the value is `i(z) • m`, a generator of `I·G`. -/
theorem multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero
    {Z : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O')}
    (g : G ⟶ Z) (hg : (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι ≫ g = 0) :
    multiplicationMap f I G i ≫ g = 0 := by
  have hker : ∀ (U : Cᵒᵖ) (m : G.val.obj U), m ∈ (imageProductSubmodule i G).obj U →
      g.val.app U m = 0 := by
    intro U m hm
    exact congrArg (fun k => k.val.app U (⟨m, hm⟩ : (imageProductSubmodule i G).obj U)) hg
  apply (PresheafOfModules.sheafificationHomEquiv
    (𝟙 (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O').obj)).injective
  apply PresheafOfModules.hom_ext
  intro U
  apply ModuleCat.MonoidalCategory.tensor_ext
  intro a x
  change g.val.app U ((multiplicationMap f I G i).val.app U
    (((PresheafOfModules.sheafificationAdjunction.{w, v, w, u}
        (R := Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O')
        (𝟙 (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O').obj)).unit.app
      (PresheafOfModules.tensorObj ((SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (ringMap f)).obj I).val
        G.val)).app U
        (a ⊗ₜ[O'.obj.obj U] x))) = 0
  rw [multiplicationMap_tmul f I G i U (show I.val.obj U from a) x]
  exact hker U _ (smul_mem_imageProductSubmodule i G U (show I.val.obj U from a) x)

/-- **A morphism out of `G` kills the multiplication map exactly when it kills the ideal
product `I·G`.** -/
theorem multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero_iff
    {Z : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O')}
    (g : G ⟶ Z) :
    multiplicationMap f I G i ≫ g = 0 ↔ (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι ≫ g = 0 :=
  ⟨imageProductSubmodule_ι_comp_eq_zero f I G i g,
    multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero f I G i g⟩

/-- **A cokernel of the inclusion of the ideal product `I·G` is a cokernel of the multiplication
map.**

This is the form in which Stacks 08MQ feeds its reduction to Stacks 08M4: the reduction
`G ⟶ i^*G` arrives as a cokernel of `I·G ⟶ G`, and 08M4 asks for a cokernel of the
multiplication map. -/
noncomputable def cokernelCofork_multiplicationMap_isColimit
    {F : SheafOfModules.{w} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O')}
    (q : G ⟶ F) (hiq : (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι ≫ q = 0)
    (hq : IsColimit (CokernelCofork.ofπ q hiq))
    (hnuq : multiplicationMap f I G i ≫ q = 0) :
    IsColimit (CokernelCofork.ofπ q hnuq) :=
  have : Epi q := Cofork.IsColimit.epi hq
  CokernelCofork.IsColimit.ofπ' q hnuq fun k hk =>
    CokernelCofork.IsColimit.desc' hq k
      (imageProductSubmodule_ι_comp_eq_zero f I G i k hk)

section IdealProductIso

variable [HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{w}]

/-- **An injective multiplication map is an isomorphism onto the ideal product `I·G`** — and the
isomorphism is one **over `G`**, carrying the multiplication map to the inclusion of `I·G`. That
second half is what makes this a statement about the canonical map of Stacks 08MQ rather than
about an abstract isomorphism of the two sources, so the two are returned together.

This is the conversion clause (2) of Stacks 08MQ needs: 08M4 delivers the injectivity of the
multiplication map, while 08MQ asks for the canonical map to `I·G` to be an isomorphism.

No sectionwise image is computed. The two maps `nu` and `I·G ⟶ G` are both monomorphisms with a
common cokernel — common by
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero.cokernelCofork_multiplicationMap_isColimit` — and in an
abelian category a monomorphism is a kernel of any of its cokernels, so both are kernels of that
one map and are therefore the same subobject of `G`. The hard inclusion, that the image of `nu`
lies in `I·G` rather than merely locally in it, never has to be proved: it is carried by the
uniqueness of a kernel. -/
noncomputable def multiplicationMapIsoIdealProduct
    (hnu : Mono (multiplicationMap f I G i)) :
    { e : ((Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.sheafTensor.{u, v, w, w, w} O').flip.obj G).obj
            ((SheafOfModules.restrictScalars (ringMap f)).obj I) ≅
          (imageProductSubmodule i G).toSheafOfModules //
        e.hom ≫ (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι = multiplicationMap f I G i } :=
  haveI := hnu
  have hiq : (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι ≫ cokernel.π (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι = 0 :=
    cokernel.condition _
  have hqc : IsColimit (CokernelCofork.ofπ (cokernel.π (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι) hiq) :=
    cokernelIsCokernel _
  let Lnu := Abelian.monoIsKernelOfCokernel
    (CokernelCofork.ofπ (cokernel.π (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι)
      (multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero f I G i _ hiq))
    (cokernelCofork_multiplicationMap_isColimit f I G i _ hiq hqc
      (multiplicationMap_comp_eq_zero f I G i _ hiq))
  let Lι := Abelian.monoIsKernelOfCokernel
    (CokernelCofork.ofπ (cokernel.π (imageProductSubmodule i G).ι) hiq) hqc
  ⟨IsLimit.conePointUniqueUpToIso Lnu Lι,
    IsLimit.conePointUniqueUpToIso_hom_comp Lnu Lι WalkingParallelPair.zero⟩

end IdealProductIso

end Stacks.SheafOfModules.FlatSquareZero

end
